Linear inequality:
-it does not have one solution but rather it has most of the time inifinite number of solutions
-we can use a number line to represent the solution set of a particular inequality or we can use interval notation
-when creating an interval notation for a solution of an enquality you always start writing your interval from left to right or in other words from smallest number to largest number.

Solving Linear Enequality:
is the same as solving linear equations except for one thing:
-if you multiply by or divide by a negative number then you have to flip the enequality symbol e.g. -x/-1 > 12/-1 then that becomes x < -12

NOTE: you reverse the greater than or the less than symbol only when you multiply or divide both sides by a negative.

Compound Inequalities:
are an equalities that are combined together using one of 2 words either the word or or the word and.

NOTE: when there is an and that combines 2 inequalities the value of the variable has to satisify both inequalities.
NOTE: with or solutions need to satisify at least one inequality.

NOTE: there is a special type of compound inequality which is called double inequality which is the same as a compound inequality and can be written in the following form:
	-3 <= x/2 + 1 < 5
so you can simply solve each inequality separaetly and then graph them togther or you can solve it at once but consider the following:
 - whatever you do to the middle part you gotta do to the other 2 sides.

Compound inequality with OR:
-the solution set can satisify the first enquality or the other inequality or both of them
-we can denote an OR between 2 intervals by using the Union symbol like this "U".

NOTE: you will always have a solution for OR

Graphing Lines:
we normally graph lines that has a linear equation in 2 variables and the linear equation can be written in 3 different forms and each form has a way to make graphing easier:
-slope-intercept form: we can use the y intercept point and the slope to graph a line without having to plot points
-standard form: we can subsitute x with 0 to get the y-intercept point and we can subsitute y with 0 to get the x-interecpt point but note that in the standard form we can use this method only if the constant term is non-zero.

NOTE: at the bear minimum we need 2 points to graph a line

NOTE: in linear equations if you have an x variable you will have an x-intercept point and if you have a y variable you will have a y-intercept point.